{
  "id": "psalm-9-10-numbering",
  "kind": "versification",
  "description": "Verse map rows for Psalms 9 and 10 between the English (eng), Masoretic/original (org) and Septuagint (lxx) numbering, stored as rows from each scheme to org as specs/19 requires, with Psalm 13:5-6 added as the nearest real merge case. Rows written as ranges expand verse by verse. The table is a fixture, not a complete versification: anything outside it is unmapped by design.",
  "license": "CC0 (numbering facts; no copyrighted text)",
  "source": "Each row carries its own source note; the eng and lxx scheme names follow Paratext usage.",
  "rows": [
    {
      "from_scheme": "eng",
      "from": "Ps.9.0",
      "to_scheme": "org",
      "to": "Ps.9.1",
      "kind": "equal",
      "source": "The Hebrew superscription of Psalm 9 is verse 1 in the MT (BHS/WLC); English editions print it unnumbered, and the Paratext eng scheme numbers an unnumbered title as verse 0. org is the Paratext/Copenhagen original scheme, which follows the Masoretic numbering with the superscription as verse 1 of Psalm 9."
    },
    {
      "from_scheme": "eng",
      "from": "Ps.9.1-Ps.9.20",
      "to_scheme": "org",
      "to": "Ps.9.2-Ps.9.21",
      "kind": "equal",
      "source": "English Psalm 9 has 20 verses, Hebrew 21: every English verse n is Hebrew verse n+1 (compare KJV/BSB Psalm 9 with WLC Psalm 9)."
    },
    {
      "from_scheme": "eng",
      "from": "Ps.10.1-Ps.10.18",
      "to_scheme": "org",
      "to": "Ps.10.1-Ps.10.18",
      "kind": "equal",
      "source": "Psalm 10 has no superscription in the MT, so English and Hebrew numbering agree (18 verses in both)."
    },
    {
      "from_scheme": "lxx",
      "from": "Ps.9.1-Ps.9.21",
      "to_scheme": "org",
      "to": "Ps.9.1-Ps.9.21",
      "kind": "equal",
      "source": "Rahlfs' Septuagint numbers the superscription as Psalm 9:1 like the MT, so Greek 9:1-21 is Hebrew 9:1-21."
    },
    {
      "from_scheme": "lxx",
      "from": "Ps.9.22-Ps.9.39",
      "to_scheme": "org",
      "to": "Ps.10.1-Ps.10.18",
      "kind": "equal",
      "source": "The Septuagint joins Hebrew Psalms 9 and 10 (one alphabetic acrostic) into Greek Psalm 9 of 39 verses: Greek 9:22-39 is Hebrew 10:1-18. Not verified against a printed Rahlfs here; the verse count (21 + 18 = 39) is consistent."
    },
    {
      "from_scheme": "eng",
      "from": "Ps.13.0",
      "to_scheme": "org",
      "to": "Ps.13.1",
      "kind": "equal",
      "source": "Psalm 13 superscription, same convention as Psalm 9. Included because Psalm 13 is the nearest real case of a verse boundary that moves inside a verse."
    },
    {
      "from_scheme": "eng",
      "from": "Ps.13.1-Ps.13.4",
      "to_scheme": "org",
      "to": "Ps.13.2-Ps.13.5",
      "kind": "equal",
      "source": "English Psalm 13:1-4 is Hebrew 13:2-5 (superscription offset)."
    },
    {
      "from_scheme": "eng",
      "from": "Ps.13.5",
      "to_scheme": "org",
      "to": "Ps.13.6",
      "kind": "merge",
      "source": "Hebrew Psalm 13:6 covers English 13:5 and 13:6 (KJV/BSB divide 'But I have trusted in Your loving devotion' from 'I will sing to the LORD'); English 13:5 is the first part of Hebrew 13:6. Mapping eng to org is therefore partial; org 13:6 to eng is exact with two targets."
    },
    {
      "from_scheme": "eng",
      "from": "Ps.13.6",
      "to_scheme": "org",
      "to": "Ps.13.6",
      "kind": "merge",
      "source": "English Psalm 13:6 is the second part of Hebrew 13:6; see the previous row."
    }
  ],
